{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' docs/DECISIONS.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Standards Control Plane — Decision Log\n\n**Last Updated:** 2026-05-09 (D-044 filed at WP-SCP-024 024B; D-045 / D-046 remain reserved; estate cascade)\n\n> **D-021 reservation note (2026-04-28):** D-021 is reserved for the\n> 2026-05-31 atomic workday filing per WP-SCP-019 hygiene response (PR\n> #30, 2026-04-21). Codex executors running WP-SCP-022 implementation\n> slices must NOT assign D-021 to any decision filed during the\n> autonomous run. The WP-SCP-022 §4.7 gate-enforcement helper\n> (`scripts/wp_scp_022_gate_check.sh --check-d021`) detects collisions.\n\n> **D-041 / D-042 / D-043 reservation note (2026-05-03, plan-doc 023A):**\n> D-041 is reserved for WP-SCP-023 slice 023B (cross-repo scorecard data\n> shape + opt-in adopter participation model). D-042 is reserved for\n> slice 023C (aggregator pipeline trust model — GitHub Actions Artifact\n> Attestation for adopter emits + SCP-side Ed25519 key registry).\n> **Mandatory trust constraint on D-042 (closes 023A R2 MAJ-SAFE-R2-001,\n> per WP-SCP-023 §5 step 3):** the 023C aggregator MUST verify the\n> `job_workflow_ref` OIDC claim in each artifact attestation against the\n> SCP reusable workflow path AND SHA pin via `gh attestation verify\n> --signer-workflow <owner>/<repo>/.github/workflows/policy-check.yml@<sha>`;\n> OIDC JWKS signature verification alone is not sufficient.\n> D-043 is reserved for slice 023D (MCP method `scp.consult_scorecard`\n> shape + read-only contract). Codex executors dispatched for any\n> WP-SCP-023 implementation slice must NOT assign D-041, D-042, or D-043\n> to any other decision filed during that slice. The reservation pattern\n> mirrors the D-021 guard above.\n\n> **D-044 / D-045 / D-046 reservation note (2026-05-03, plan-doc 024A):**\n> D-044 is reserved for WP-SCP-024 slice 024B (`scripts/scaffold-downstream.sh`\n> = `SCP-073-scaffolder` operational contract + adopter-template versioning\n> + invocation-log convention extension + `enable-required-check.sh\n> --restore` rollback mode). D-045 is reserved for slice 024C (estate\n> cascade ordering canary-first per WP-SCP-024 §5.1, per-adopter\n> onboarding contract per §5.2, post-bake observation window ≥1 calendar\n> week + ≥1 Renovate bump cycle per invariant 8, cascade-slice rollback\n> procedure per invariant 7). D-046 is reserved for slice 024G\n> (estate-cascade Threshold A criteria + post-Threshold-A maintenance\n> posture: recurring drift remediation, version-ramp coordination,\n> departing-adopter procedure; decision on whether `scp.consult_estate_status`\n> ships standalone or rolls into existing `consult_scorecard`). Codex\n> executors dispatched for any WP-SCP-024 implementation slice must NOT\n> assign D-044, D-045, or D-046 to any other decision filed during that\n> slice. The reservation pattern mirrors the D-021 / D-041–043 guards\n> above.\n\n| ID | Date | Decision | Status | Rationale |\n|----|------|----------|--------|-----------|\n| D-001 | 2026-04-11 | Use Python for the phase 1 CLI-first implementation | ACCEPTED | Fast path to explicit schemas, local tooling, JSON validation, and deterministic file-based execution |\n| D-002 | 2026-04-11 | Keep the project as a standalone app rather than extending docs-agent or Control Tower directly | ACCEPTED | Keeps evaluator runtime independent while still allowing later integration with both systems |\n| D-003 | 2026-04-11 | Limit the first governed implementation slice to standards registry loading, minimal findings loading, and consult retrieval | ACCEPTED | Keeps the first end-to-end work package narrow enough to review rigorously while still allowing real consult output |\n| D-004 | 2026-04-11 | Introduce an explicit project-area intermediate representation before evaluator implementation | ACCEPTED | Keeps evaluator inputs deterministic, testable, and independent from raw repo sprawl |\n| D-005 | 2026-04-11 | Wire governance evaluation through the audit CLI before attempting architecture evaluation | ACCEPTED | Establishes one real evaluator path end to end before widening the audit surface |\n| D-006 | 2026-04-11 | Extend audit and project-area contracts before WP-SCP-003 implementation | ACCEPTED | Governance review evidence and unsupported-domain coverage must be first-class structured data, not implied by prose or raw path scanning |\n| D-007 | 2026-04-11 | Use an explicit unattended-delivery protocol for the remaining programme | ACCEPTED | The remaining work should continue without waiting for user answers, using documented default decisions, bounded review loops, and backlog carry-forward for non-blocking residuals |\n| D-008 | 2026-04-12 | Use explicit file-content signal heuristics for phase-1 architecture evaluation | ACCEPTED | The architecture slice needs deterministic, test-backed checks against repo-local evidence rather than broad semantic inference or prompt-only judgement |\n| D-009 | 2026-04-12 | Add a second repo-backed fixture corpus for architecture signal coverage | ACCEPTED | `ARCH-003` and `ARCH-004` need concrete repo-backed sample files instead of synthetic payload-only tests if the slice is going to prove its own honesty |\n| D-010 | 2026-04-12 | Reconcile findings by deterministic finding ID and area-scoped audit coverage | ACCEPTED | Findings persistence must refresh and resolve records only inside the audited area and evaluated domains so unrelated history is not disturbed |\n| D-011 | 2026-04-12 | Guard findings persistence with scope-collision checks and per-file atomic replacement plus rollback | ACCEPTED | Persisted lifecycle state must fail on `finding_id` scope collisions and should avoid half-written files without pretending phase 1 has a crash-safe multi-file transaction |\n| D-012 | 2026-04-12 | Bind generated report and area-summary timestamps to the current audit timestamp | ACCEPTED | Report outputs need byte-stable reruns and freshness checks, so they should reuse `audit_result.generated_at` rather than inventing wall-clock timestamps |\n| D-013 | 2026-04-12 | Reuse the current write-flow guarantees for report artifacts and carry true bundled transactions to backlog | ACCEPTED | The reporting slice should stay downstream of the existing persistence guarantees instead of pretending to offer a crash-safe cross-artifact commit in phase 1 |\n| D-014 | 2026-04-12 | Guard `report` freshness with embedded findings-state signatures as well as audit timestamp | ACCEPTED | A date-bound audit timestamp alone is too weak to detect same-day content drift, so the report needs a stronger freshness check against persisted findings state |\n| D-015 | 2026-04-12 | Apply waivers as an audit-time overlay rather than embedding waiver rules inside evaluators | ACCEPTED | Evaluators should keep emitting deterministic raw findings so the same checks can be reused with or without waivers, while audit assembly owns exception handling |\n| D-016 | 2026-04-12 | Centralise score calculation in a shared module and count only active unwaived findings against score | ACCEPTED | Score semantics must be documented, deterministic, and consistent across evaluators and audit assembly, and waived issues should not drag active score down |\n| D-017 | 2026-04-12 | Use one embedded fenced JSON review-evidence block as the shared contract for governance validation and historical review retrieval | ACCEPTED | One small structured block inside markdown keeps review evidence inspectable in git while avoiding two drifting review-data formats |\n| D-018 | 2026-04-12 | Keep historical review retrieval as explicit consult output rather than hiding it inside generic guidance text | ACCEPTED | Review history should be visible, bounded, and machine-readable so later consumers can use it without reparsing prose |\n| D-019 | 2026-04-18 | SVC-003 is declare-a-contract with a closed four-mode set (`mode.user_oidc`, `mode.service_rs256`, `mode.api_key`, `mode.bearer_legacy`); commit `66ba8a4` codifies `mode.user_oidc` with no rework; WP-SCP-018 `--auth-token` classifies as `mode.bearer_legacy` with deprecation close date 2026-06-30 | ACCEPTED | Estate reality has three live token types plus a cookie session flow; a single fixed mechanism breaks machine callers; a closed mode set prevents drift; codifying the existing CT OIDC layer keeps the in-repo auth story consistent without forcing a rework |\n| D-020 | 2026-04-18 | `_normalise_scope` accepts `request.scope.area_id` as the normaliser hint when `_infer_area_id` raises `AreaIdInferenceError`; mismatched-inference still raises the same way as before | ACCEPTED | Adopters auditing scopes that lack an ENH-style marker (e.g. a root-level `services.yml` dogfood) would otherwise have to plant placeholder enhancement docs to satisfy area-id inference; the narrow relaxation preserves the auditor discipline of catching inferred-vs-claimed mismatches while closing the genuine uninferrable-scope ergonomic gap |\n| D-022 | 2026-04-21 | Adopt reusable GitHub Actions workflow + OPA/Conftest + Renovate shared preset + required status check with `enforce_admins=true` as the estate's policy federation primitive. Alternatives rejected: `palantir/policy-bot` (GitHub App per org, HCL-not-Rego, no Renovate cascade, no native waiver-overlay); custom control-plane service (build cost, duplicates GH Actions + branch protection primitives) | ACCEPTED | Industry-standard shape; no custom control-plane service required; cheapest path converting SCP from advisory auditor to pre-merge gate; Renovate preset cascade gives \"change one rule → enforce across 10 repos in 24h\"; supports waiver-aware evaluation and structured annotation payloads. Full rationale body in `docs/plans/WP-SCP-020-policy-federation-primitive.md` §10 |\n| D-023 | 2026-04-21 | Reject multi-agent free-form chat-forum pattern as the agent-coordination venue; adopt structured proposal queue (`docs/reviews/proposals/PROP-NNN.md` + MCP `propose()` tool — initial stub in WP-SCP-021 slice 021E; full adjudication workflow in `WP-SCP-022-proposal-queue`, separately tracked per `docs/plans/WP-SCP-022-implementation-programme-plan.md` §12). Agents never free-form chat with each other; they read SCP's decision records and call SCP's MCP tools | ACCEPTED | Du et al. (ICML 2024, `arxiv 2305.14325`) show multi-agent debate converges to consensus fast including when the consensus is wrong; Anthropic's multi-agent research post reports ~15× token cost with compounding coordination errors; human-on-the-loop literature converges on moderator-as-bottleneck past a few decisions/hour. Proposal-queue pattern scales because the human is on the loop, not in it. Full rationale body in `docs/plans/WP-SCP-020-policy-federation-primitive.md` §10 (decision originator) and `docs/plans/WP-SCP-021-scp-as-mcp-server.md` §10 (`propose()` stub design). Note 2026-04-28: original D-023 forward-referenced \"WP-SCP-022 plan when that WP opens\"; WP-SCP-022 v0.2 is the implementation programme plan, not the proposal-queue WP — the rationale-body expansion lives in the parent plans named here, and the adjudication WP is renamed `WP-SCP-022-proposal-queue` |\n| D-024 | 2026-04-21 | Adopt Anthropic's Model Context Protocol (MCP) as the estate's agent-to-SCP tool protocol. Transports: stdio (primary, local, trust-the-process) + streamable HTTP (opt-in, bearer-token via `SCP_MCP_TOKEN` with 30-day rotation overlap). Ed25519 asymmetric signing for consult receipts — SCP holds private key; adopters verify with public key published at `scp://security/signing-keys` + committed `docs/security/mcp-signing-keys.pub` (90-day rotation overlap). Python `mcp` SDK pinned by version + sdist SHA256; distributed via PyPI as `standards-control-plane[mcp]` extra. OAuth deferred to SCP-075-oauth | ACCEPTED | MCP is the dominant vertical agent-to-tool protocol post Linux Foundation Agentic AI Foundation launch (Dec 2025); Anthropic is protocol origin; Claude Code/Desktop + ACC speak MCP natively. Asymmetric signing prevents adopter-held-key forgery (HMAC symmetric rejected in adversarial review round 1). Alternatives rejected: raw REST (no client ecosystem); custom JSON-RPC (reinvents MCP); gRPC (no agent client support); HMAC symmetric (forgeable by any key-holding adopter). Full rationale in `docs/plans/WP-SCP-021-scp-as-mcp-server.md` §10 |\n| D-025 | 2026-04-21 | Scoped pre-code consult requirement with Ed25519-signed receipts. Downstream `CLAUDE.md` mandates `scp.consult_rules` before code authoring in governed domains; Claude Code `PreCommit` hook refuses commit without valid receipt. Scope rule: hook consults `scp://rules/domain-map` via `resolve_domain(changed_files)`; non-empty result → receipt required; default-skip globs for docs-only changes. Receipt envelope binds `{schema_version, key_id, repo, head_sha, base_sha, changed_files_hash, domains_covered, issued_at, expires_at ≤ 2h}`; hook validates every field. Break-glass `SCP_MCP_ALLOW_OFFLINE=true` requires committed `docs/overrides/OVERRIDE-NNN.md` with schema-validated expiry ≤ 14d; emits tracked finding SCP-MCP-E010 | ACCEPTED | Client-side enforcement pairs with server-side gate (WP-SCP-020) as belt + braces; scoped + TTL + PR-bound receipts prevent replay/forgery/scope-bypass. Break-glass requires sibling OVERRIDE artefact so fail-closed posture is maintained. Rego gate is authoritative — MCP consult never bypasses. Full rationale in `docs/plans/WP-SCP-021-scp-as-mcp-server.md` §10 |\n| D-026 | 2026-04-28 | Autonomous-dispatch protocol for SCP implementation slices. Each WP-SCP-020 and WP-SCP-021 implementation slice flows through four-tier dispatch: Opus orchestrator (current agent) prepares dispatch package conforming to `~/Projects/acc/schemas/codex_work_package.schema.json`; Codex executor tier runs the slice via `codex_dispatch.py`; 3× parallel Sonnet R1 reviewers apply correctness / safety_bypass / completeness_governance lenses via `claude_dispatch.py` with 500 ms stagger; Opus consolidates verdicts and orchestrates fix rounds. Recurse to fixpoint per `feedback_recursive_adversarial_review.md`. Fix-round budget 5; on budget exhaustion, slice escalates to user review. Evidence persists at `docs/reviews/WP-SCP-022/dispatches/<slice-id>/`. No slice merges without 3× APPROVED verdict | ACCEPTED | Estate four-tier dispatch standard since 2026-04-22 (per `feedback_four_tier_dispatch.md`); applies to non-trivial work. SCP implementation slices are non-trivial — they touch CI, branch protection, signing keys, and PyPI. Adversarial review pattern proven on WP-SCP-019 (5 rounds), WP-SCP-020 (5 rounds), WP-SCP-021 (3 rounds, fixpoint). Persisting evidence in-repo gives a permanent audit trail and lets future agents recover from session-bounded amnesia. Full rationale in `docs/plans/WP-SCP-022-implementation-programme-plan.md` §4 |\n| D-027 | 2026-04-28 | Parallel-track execution for WP-SCP-020 (federation primitive) and WP-SCP-021 (MCP server) implementation, Scope Y per user 2026-04-28 selection. Both tracks run concurrently from dispatch-zero to user-gate. Track 1 (020): 020B → 020B.1 → 020B.2 → 020C → 020C.1 → 020J → 020K → 020D1 → 020H part 1 → 020E.a → [USER-GATE-A0 release sign-off] → 020H part 2 → 020D2 → [USER-GATE-A] (12 implementation slices + 1 mid-chain human-input gate + 1 terminal gate, matching WP-SCP-020 §3 canonical sequence verbatim). Track 2 (021): 021B → 021C → 021D → 021E → [USER-GATE-C] (4 implementation slices + 1 terminal gate). Concurrent-dispatch limit: 4 active Codex sessions across both tracks. `pyproject.toml` partition: Track 1 owns `[tool.scp.federation]` and adds `scp-policy-check` to `[project.scripts]`; Track 2 owns `[project.optional-dependencies.mcp]` and adds `scp-mcp-server` to `[project.scripts]`. `docs/STATUS.md` excluded from all slice scope_boundary globs | ACCEPTED | WP-SCP-021 §scope statement declares the tracks independent; serial execution would defer MCP availability for ~2× wall-clock without quality gain. Concurrent dispatch within rate-limit envelope per `four-tier-dispatch-field-report-2026-04-22.md`. Full rationale in `docs/plans/WP-SCP-022-implementation-programme-plan.md` §4.4. Track 1 sequence corrected in v0.2 (R1 fix-round) to match WP-SCP-020 §3 canonical ordering — earlier draft omitted 020K and inverted 020D2 placement |\n| D-028 | 2026-04-28 | SCP adopts D-048 / ADR-016 Design Parity Build Method (DPBM) as a contract input for any SCP slice that produces designed visual output. Layered amendment to four-tier dispatch — prototype + flows + design principles enter the contract layer the build is bound to. Enforcement-injection mechanism per `docs/plans/WP-SCP-022-implementation-programme-plan.md` §4.6: visual-output slices' dispatch packages MUST include a \"DPBM contract\" subsection naming prototype path, flow diagram, and design-principles reference; orchestrator verifies subsection presence before launching the review pass; absence is treated as a CRIT finding. Within WP-SCP-022 itself no slices match the DPBM \"applies\" criteria (workflow / Rego / MCP-server / CI work, no visual output); D-028 is forward-looking. WP-SCP-024 estate cascade inherits D-028; FLA, ACC, RI canary-onboarding instructions reference the DPBM contract layer. D-028 lands operationally once CT PR #202 + ACC PR #106 have merged | ACCEPTED | DPBM closes a structural gap that pure-code lenses can't see — a frontend built under standard four-tier dispatch can satisfy every test and still drift from the design. Without an SCP-side adoption decision, agents working through SCP's MCP consult won't be told that the contract for visual-output work includes design artefacts. CT PR #202 + ACC PR #106 (both 2026-04-28) introduce the upstream doctrine; SCP adopts at the policy layer. Full rationale + injection-mechanism specification in `docs/plans/WP-SCP-022-implementation-programme-plan.md` §4.6 |\n| D-029 | 2026-04-29 | `policy-check.yml` reusable workflow `permissions:` block expands to `contents: read, statuses: write` to support WP-SCP-022 020C.1(vi) read-back commit-status posting on the sibling context `scp/policy-check-readback`. Original 020B(iii) baseline was `contents: read` only. The `statuses: write` privilege uses the caller's `${{ github.token }}` and posts only to `repos/<caller>/statuses/${{ pull_request.head.sha }}`; no other write surface is unlocked. Disabling the read-back would require accepting a regression on 020C.1(vi); keeping `contents: read` only would prevent the read-back from being posted | ACCEPTED | The read-back surface is the only place a PR reviewer sees waiver/disabled-rule posture without reading JSON artefacts; WP-SCP-020 §4.3 fixpoint criteria for 020C.1(vi) require it. The privilege footprint is narrowest possible — `statuses: write` does not allow content modification, branch operations, issue/PR comments, or token impersonation. The workflow checkout uses `persist-credentials: false` so the elevated privilege is bounded to the policy-check job. Plan amendment: `docs/plans/WP-SCP-020-policy-federation-primitive.md` §4 020B(iii) updated with cross-reference to D-029. Identified by WP-SCP-022 R1 completeness review (MAJ-005) |\n| D-030 | 2026-04-30 | Apply WP-SCP-020 slice 020J on the SCP repository: (a) tag-protection rule on `v*` (legacy tag-protection API and/or Repository Ruleset) preventing force-push or deletion of release tags; (b) `required-signed-commits` on `main` so every commit reaching `main` carries a verified signature. Both are preconditions for slice 020D1 (self-dogfood wrapper pinned at the v1.0.0-rc.1 commit-SHA) and 020D2 (required-status-check + v1.0.0 cut). Configured via `scripts/configure-020j-protections.sh` (idempotent API caller). Documented in `docs/security/branch-protection.md`. Bus-factor-1 acknowledged: a single GitHub admin (@jrnb2024) can disable these via repo Settings; quarterly review cadence per WP-SCP-020 §14 U-sec-2 closure | ACCEPTED | Path (a) selected at WP-SCP-020 plan merge per §14 U-sec-2 — Personal GitHub Pro tier supports both protections natively, no signed-release-manifest substitute needed. Tag-protection prevents tag mutation post-cut (the supply-chain anchor for adopters pinning by SHA + comment-tag); required-signed-commits prevents un-attested code from reaching the gate's source of truth. Future direct commits to main without a verified signature are blocked, which is the intent (forces all changes through PRs whose squash-merges are auto-signed by GitHub's bot key). The unsigned commit `bcfc706` (USER-GATE-C signoff, 2026-04-29, unsigned) predates this enablement and is grandfathered. Identified as gate-readiness work in `docs/plans/WP-SCP-020-policy-federation-primitive.md` §4 020J + §3 sequence + §8 risks (\"Bootstrap trust\") + §9.1 sequencing |\n| D-031 | 2026-04-30 | Apply WP-SCP-020 slice 020K on the SCP repository in the personal-account / single-operator mode resolved at §14 U-k closure: (a) NO `scp-break-glass` GitHub team is created — GitHub teams are not available on user namespaces and `jrnb2024` is a personal user account; (b) `CODEOWNERS` extends to cover the additional governance-sensitive paths named in §4 020K (canonical-minimum), then broadened/extended via 020K R1+R2+R3 safety reviews to close 7 MAJ + 4 MIN + 6 nit findings — see CODEOWNERS file header + `FIX-ROUND-{1,2,3}.md` for the full audit trail of which paths were broadened (subsuming a narrower canonical rule) vs added net-new; (c) at slice 020D2, branch-protection on `main` is configured with `required_approving_review_count = 1`, `dismiss_stale_reviews: true`, and `require_review_from_non_author = false` (solo operator; self-approval is the only option) — **NOTE (D-033 amendment, 2026-04-30 evening):** the anticipated `required_approving_review_count = 1` was corrected to `count = 0` by D-033 when the first PR through enforced mode revealed that GitHub forbids PR self-review; count=1 + sole CODEOWNER = operator locked out of repo. See D-033 for the live state, the operational test path (USER-GATE-A signoff PR #64), and the second-maintainer escalation path; (d) §8 bus-factor-1 risk row remains LIVE with quarterly escalation review at 2026-07-21 named in `STATUS.md` \"Open scheduled follow-ups\" and in auto-memory `project_scheduled_followups.md`. **Escalation path when a second maintainer onboards:** add them to `CODEOWNERS`, flip `require_review_from_non_author = true`, amend §8 risk row to CLOSED. **020D1 → 020D2 dependency:** 020K completes before 020D2 opens (the branch-protection wiring at 020D2 reads from CODEOWNERS established here). Identified at WP-SCP-020 plan §3 sequence + §4 020K + §14 U-k closure | ACCEPTED | Personal-account path is the only viable shape for v1.0.0 on a personal user namespace. The CODEOWNERS expansion forces every future change to `renovate/**` (Renovate preset that cascades pin-bumps across adopters), `docs/DECISIONS.md` (the decision-record canonical home), and `output/findings/waivers.json` (the safety-valve store) through reviewer attention rather than allowing direct push. Bus-factor-1 is acknowledged as live operational exposure rather than papered over: `require_review_from_non_author = false` reflects reality (a solo operator cannot self-approve under non-author review), and the 2026-07-21 quarterly review is the named re-evaluation moment. Path (b) extension of `CODEOWNERS` is forward-looking on `renovate/**` (the directory does not yet exist in the working tree; it lands in slice 020F); CODEOWNERS rules on non-existent paths are no-ops, so adding the rule pre-emptively is harmless and removes a future drift risk where 020F could land without simultaneously adding the matching CODEOWNERS row. Full rationale in `docs/plans/WP-SCP-020-policy-federation-primitive.md` §4 020K + §8 (bus factor risk row, line 188) + §14 U-k closure |\n| D-032 | 2026-04-30 | Apply WP-SCP-020 slice 020D2 on the SCP repository post-USER-GATE-A0: enable `scp/policy-check` as a required status check on `main` with `strict: true` (PR must be up-to-date with base before evaluation); set `enforce_admins: true` (no admin bypass); set `required_pull_request_reviews` with `required_approving_review_count: 1`, `dismiss_stale_reviews: true`, `require_code_owner_reviews: true`; explicitly leave `require_review_from_non_author: false` per 020K personal-account closure (single-operator self-approval is the only viable path). Configured via `scripts/configure-020d2-required-check.sh` (idempotent). After 020D2, every PR to `main` must pass the federation primitive's gate AND have at least one approving review AND have a verified signature on every commit AND cannot be bypassed by the admin role. The 020E.a pre-protection canary (workflow-run-id 25172373569) demonstrated the deny path on a real PR with a real violation pre-flip; 020E.b will demonstrate that the now-required check actually blocks merge. Bus-factor-1 acknowledged: a single GitHub admin (@jrnb2024) is the operator; quarterly review at 2026-07-21 (per 020K + D-031) reconciles documented-vs-live state. **Note (D-033 forward-reference, 2026-04-30 evening):** the live state of three D-032 parameters was amended same-day by D-033 — the required-check context name corrected from `scp/policy-check` to `policy-check / scp/policy-check` (rendered Actions name); `required_approving_review_count` corrected from 1 to 0 (single-operator self-approval is structurally impossible on GitHub); `require_code_owner_reviews` corrected from true to false (same root cause). See D-033 for the operational test path that surfaced these | ACCEPTED | This is the operational closure of the WP-SCP-020 federation primitive — SCP gates itself on its own `main`. Closure of governance B-2 (the original brief was \"convert SCP from advisory auditor to deterministic pre-merge enforcement\"). Required-status-check + enforce_admins=